unway TaihuLight, which is the fastest supercomputer at present.&nbsp;</p>"}, {"time": "40000", "image": "enpe8n4le01ob12re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Now then, regarding the evolution of supercomputers, the first supercomputer ever mentioned was the Atlas, back in the 60&rsquo;s. The electrical engineer Seymour Cray designed it in 1962, but later in 1964, he had enough experience when he conceived the Data CDC 6600, which had silicon transistors and ran at 10 MHz. It was popular but not enough as the Cray I was, which soon became a perfect replacement for the CDC 6600 in 1976. This one had a more modern architecture, integrated circuits, and a vector processing. However, regarding parallelism, this was less functional than the next supercomputer, the Cray X-MP. This new version was better in terms of parallelism thanks to the incorporation of multiple processors.&nbsp;</p>"}, {"time": "101000", "image": "enpe8n4le01ob12re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">On the other hand, supercomputers were less common in the market since these were more expensive than the usual ones. The reason is that Supercomputers are only used for special and advanced purposes. Consequently, engineers are constantly creating new inventions in order to improve the existing versions and offer solutions to any challenge that science and new generations impose. As supercomputers change almost every day, it is not enough for engineers; so, they have to develop new machines that can help with aircraft prototypes, the analysis of nuclear effects, the weather models, and other important projects, such as the National Security Agency or NSA that controls communication all around the world.</p>"}], "coverimg": "enpe8n4le01ob12re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"video_src": "enpe8n4le01ob12re5vsc01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image